        "summary": "Retirement planning in India has undergone a significant transformation across generations, with parents' retirement investments largely relying on bank fixed deposits, pensions, and savings accounts. However, for the last two decades, investors have increasingly invested in market-linked products like mutual funds and the National Pension System (NPS). However, this does not necessarily guarantee a safe retirement due to concerns about market volatility, inflation, longevity, and whether the accumulated corpus will generate steady income when regular salary stops. The comparison includes comparing returns on FDs versus SIPs or pensions, as well as looking at which generation has safer retirement planning and what modern investors may learn from how their parents prepared for their golden years. For government employees covered under the Old Pension Scheme (OPS), the situation was more comfortable because they received a defined pension equal to 50% of their last drawn salary.",
        "shortSummary": "Retirement planning in India has evolved significantly, with fewer defined-benefit pensions and fewer market-linked investments, but risks remain due to inflation and market volatility.",
