        "summary": "Gold prices have remained under pressure this week due to multiple bearish factors including sticky inflation, economic resilience, and Fed rate hike expectations. US Treasury yields continued to rise, with the 30-year yield reaching its highest in over 20 years and the 10-year level at the key 5% level. The New York Fed President, New York President Williams, has confirmed that the Fed cannot ignore short-term inflation disturbances and must instead be alert to persistent supply shocks that could further entrench inflation expectations. The probability of a Fed rate rise in October has surpassed 60% according to market pricing. Recent US economic data continues to support economic resilience and the market expects 100,000 new non-farm jobs in September and the unemployment rate to remain low, showing that the labor market is generally robust. However, August US durable goods orders showed structural strength, highlighting the resilience of corporate investment demand. The current gold trend aligns with the principle of a spring-like rebound due to the more bearish pressure beforehand and the longer it lasts, the greater the rebound once data materializes.",
